window.docContents[191].resolve({"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quiver-___D_4___/Observation-that-number-of-indecomposables-is-orientation-independent/#Chapter6___Discussion_after_Example6___3___1":{"contents":"It appears as if the number of indecomposable representations does not depend on the orientation of the edges — and indeed Gabriel's theorem will generalize this observation.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.3. Indecomposable representations of the quiver $D_4$\tObservation that number of indecomposables is orientation-independent","header":"Observation that number of indecomposables is orientation-independent","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quiver-___D_4___/Observation-that-number-of-indecomposables-is-orientation-independent/#Chapter6___Discussion_after_Example6___3___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/Section-5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___10":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.10. Frobenius reciprocity\tSection 5.10: Frobenius reciprocity","header":"Section 5.10: Frobenius reciprocity","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/Section-5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___10"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Dual-representation/#Chapter3___Definition3___3___2":{"contents":"Definition 3.3.2 (Dual representation). Let V be a representation of any algebra A. Then the dual representation V^* is the representation of the opposite algebra A^{\\mathrm{op}} (or, equivalently, right A-module) with the action\n\n(f \\cdot a)(v) := f(av).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\tDual representation","header":"Dual representation","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Dual-representation/#Chapter3___Definition3___3___2"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Adjacency-matrix-of-graph-with-nonabelian-automorphism-group-has-repeated-eigenvalues/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___4":{"contents":"Problem 4.12.4. Recall that the adjacency matrix of a graph \\Gamma (without multiple edges) is the matrix in which the ijth entry is 1 if the vertices i and j are connected with an edge, and zero otherwise. Let \\Gamma be a finite graph whose automorphism group is nonabelian. Show that the adjacency matrix of \\Gamma must have repeated eigenvalues.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.12. Problems\tAdjacency matrix of graph with nonabelian automorphism group has repeated eigenvalues","header":"Adjacency matrix of graph with nonabelian automorphism group has repeated eigenvalues","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Adjacency-matrix-of-graph-with-nonabelian-automorphism-group-has-repeated-eigenvalues/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___4"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Section-4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___9___heading-1":{"contents":"Character tables allow us to compute the tensor product multiplicities N^k_{ij} using\n\nV_i \\otimes V_j = \\sum N^k_{ij} V_k, \\quad N^k_{ij} = (\\chi_i \\chi_j, \\chi_k).\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.9. Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables\tSection 4.9: Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables","header":"4.9. Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Section-4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___9___heading-1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___6":{"contents":"Now pick V \\in N such that \\chi_V(g) \\neq 0; it exists by Lemma 5.4.7. Theorem 5.4.4 implies that g (and hence any element of C) acts by a scalar in V. Now let H be the subgroup of G generated by elements ab^{-1}, a, b \\in C. It is normal and acts trivially in V, so H \\neq G, as V is nontrivial. Also H \\neq 1, since |C| > 1. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.4. Burnside's theorem\tCompletion of proof of Theorem 5.4.6","header":"Completion of proof of Theorem 5.4.6","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___6"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/#chapter-07___section-7-7":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories","header":"7.7. Abelian categories","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/#chapter-07___section-7-7"}});
